#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The session broker: persona sessions, started on the host, reached by an app.

**Why this is a runtime component and not an app's code.** It holds the same
authority bin/wake holds -- it launches a persona in that persona's own
workspace with the real login at ``~/.claude``, subject to that persona's own
settings and hooks. An unprivileged project engineer's job must never be able
to change how a persona is launched, so the launcher lives here, under the
platform engineer, and an app gets a narrow authenticated interface to it and
nothing else.

**What a broker session is.** Exactly what a conversation is today, and
nothing added: ``claude -p`` with the persona's workspace as the working
directory, no ``--settings`` override, no appended system prompt, and no
``--model`` unless this chat has asked for one (the + menu, 2026-09-21; the
default is still nothing on the launch line). That is the whole trick. The
persona's ``.claude/settings.json``
then supplies the model, ``outputStyle: conversation`` (so ``_active.md``
loads and ``_reflection.md`` does not; for an agent with a map, since
2026-11-18, ``none``, and its map loads as a CLAUDE.md import on every
lane: lib/loam_loading.py), the permission rules, and the three
hooks -- transcript capture on Stop and SessionEnd, the boundary guard on
every PreToolUse. ``LOAM_WAKE`` is deliberately NOT set: the transcript hook
defaults to "conversation" when it is absent, and a session a person is
talking to is a conversation.

**The turn shape: one process per session, not one per turn.** With
``--input-format stream-json`` the CLI reads newline-delimited user messages
from stdin for as long as it is alive and emits its events on stdout, so a
second message goes into the same live process with its context already in
memory. The alternative -- one ``claude -p --resume`` per turn -- re-reads
the session file, re-loads CLAUDE.md and the MCP servers, and pays a cold
start every turn; measured elsewhere on this box, MCP startup alone is
seconds. It also cannot stream a partial message back while the turn is
running in any useful way. Cost: a live process holds ~450 MB, which is what
MAX_SESSIONS exists to bound, and a session that outlives the client's
interest is ended on the idle timer rather than held forever.

**Permission prompts** ride the same channel. ``--permission-prompt-tool
stdio`` (the flag the Agent SDK passes when a host supplies ``canUseTool``;
it is not in ``--help`` and was found by causing it) turns a prompt into a
``control_request`` with subtype ``can_use_tool`` on stdout, which this
forwards to the client and answers with a ``control_response`` carrying the
client's decision. Nothing about the persona's own permission settings
changes: they are what decides whether a prompt happens at all, and the
request carries the CLI's own ``decision_reason``.

**The event vocabulary is the CLI's.** Every message the CLI emits is passed
through verbatim with ``seq`` added, which is what makes reconnection
possible -- and, on a ``result`` only, ``context``, the turn's two token
figures, which are the broker's arithmetic on the CLI's own numbers and are
on the event that closes a turn so a page can draw them as the answer lands.
The broker adds six types of its own, all prefixed ``broker.``:
``broker.session`` (the session's own facts, sent first on every stream),
``broker.user_message`` (what a person sent, which the CLI never echoes),
``broker.permission_request``, ``broker.permission_resolved``,
``broker.background_tasks``, ``broker.exit``.
Inventing a second vocabulary for messages the CLI already emits would mean
maintaining a translation forever.

**``broker.session`` is a snapshot and not an event**, which is the one
distinction on this stream worth stating twice. It is not numbered, not
logged, and carries no ``seq``: its position field is ``record_seq``. It
used to carry ``seq``, meaning the number of the last event already in the
log -- and a client that treats ``seq`` as an event's identity (which is
what it is, and what the README tells a client to do) then discarded the
real event bearing that number, losing the last event of every session it
opened, and reconnected at the END of the log after dropping mid-replay.
Found by counterfactual 2026-09-16 on a real 144-event log. So every ``seq``
on this stream is an event's identity and nothing else, and the snapshot
says where the session is under a name that cannot be mistaken for one.
It is re-sent whenever a turn record is written, so a live client sees the
turn's ``cost_delta_usd`` and ``model`` without re-fetching the record.

**What a row on a screen is made of** (2026-09-18). The record grew the
fields the designed home screen draws from: ``title`` (the transcript
writer's own titler, so the app and the record agree on one name for one
conversation, standing in on the first message's opening words until it
answers), ``status`` (``working`` / ``waiting`` / ``needs_input`` / ``done`` /
``failed`` / ``idle`` -- what the session *is*, where ``state`` is only
whether a process exists), ``last_message``, ``last_event_at``, ``pinned``, ``archived`` and
``provider``, ``model`` and ``effort``. Pin and archive live here rather than in a browser so they
follow a person between devices, and ``PATCH /v1/sessions/<id>`` answers for
a session whose process ended weeks ago, which is most of a home screen.
``GET /v1/sessions/events`` is the live list: the same snapshot, whenever any
record is written, fed from ``Session.save()`` so that no field added later
can be silently left out of it. ``GET /v1/providers`` says which vendor
accounts exist, which are connected, what the quota is -- read from the
``rate_limit_event`` the CLI volunteers mid-turn, which is the only place it
is stated -- and what each offers a chat: its models and its effort levels.
All of it, and why, is in broker/README.md.

**A record older than those fields is filled from its own log** (2026-09-18,
the same evening). ``normalise`` fills the keys and cannot fill the content,
so every session started before that morning drew as "New chat" with no
status box -- caused in a real browser. The durable log holds both sides of
the conversation, so ``backfill_records()`` reads a title, a last message
and a status out of it once, at start, and writes them back. It also moves a
record whose position is behind its own log up to the log's end, because a
record that says less than its log renumbers from there on a resume.

**The conversations the owner had in the app are sessions on this box**
(2026-09-20). Claude Code on claude.ai/code and on their phone was the Remote
Control servers here, until they were retired on 2026-11-19: same login, same persona workspace, same hooks, same
session file under ``~/.claude/projects/``. So they are not imported, they
are adopted -- a record is written for each and the conversation stays in
the CLI's own file, which is read and never written to
(``lib/loam_adopt.py``).

**And that file is what such a session replays** (2026-09-20, the same day).
``adoption.iter_events`` translates it into this interface's own event
vocabulary -- ``broker.user_message`` for each turn a person typed, one
``assistant`` event per content block the way the CLI streams them, ``user``
for a tool result, and a ``result``-shaped boundary per turn carrying what
the file knows and ``null`` where it knows no cost. Thinking and subagent
traffic are excluded. The translation is a pure function of the file and is
written down nowhere: the app's file stays the only copy of that
conversation on this box, and opening the chat re-reads it. The record's
``replayed`` says how many events came from it, and ``record_seq`` is that
same number, so the first turn taken here later is numbered after the
history rather than on top of it. See ``Session.replay``.

**And sending into one forks it** (2026-09-20, the same day again). An
adopted session is never resumed in place: its id is the app's session id,
and ``claude --resume`` on it would put a second process on a file the
Remote Control server could still have been writing. So the first launch of such a
record is ``claude -p --resume <the app's id> --fork-session``, which reads
that file and writes to a new one of the CLI's own. The record keeps the
app's id as its identity -- the row, the log, the URL and the replayed
history are all under it -- and gains two fields: ``cli_session_id``, the id
the CLI minted for the fork and the one every later launch resumes, and
``forked_from``, the app session it was forked out of. The app's own file is
read and never written to, before the fork and after it. See
``Session.argv`` and ``Session.start``.

**Working means working, and a turn is not over while something is still
coming** (2026-09-22). The owner watched a chat go to its done state with the
persona still working. The cause, read off this box's own logs: the persona
launched a background subagent, the CLI closed the turn with ``result``, and
twenty minutes later the subagent's notification opened another turn with no
message from anybody. So the session is held off ``done`` while a background
task is live, while one has left the CLI's list without notifying yet, and
from a notification until the result that answers it; ``system``/``init``
reopens the turn, because the CLI writes one ahead of every turn and not once
per process. ``background_tasks`` on the record is the count, and
``broker.background_tasks`` says when it changes. See ``working_behind``.

**And the held state has its own word: ``waiting``** (2026-09-25). The hold
above was right and stays; what it got wrong was calling both halves of it
``working``. The owner watched their own chat while its agent had ended its turn
and was blocked on two background jobs: nothing was being generated, the
page's working cues had stopped, and the row read as finished. An agent that
is generating and an agent that has finished its turn and is only waiting on
its children are two states a person reads differently. So ``working`` is the
CLI's turn being open, ``waiting`` is the turn's ``result`` having arrived
with background work still outstanding, and ``done`` is nothing further
coming. One field, not a count the page has to interpret: ``background_tasks``
is separate and says what is being waited on. See ``Session.status``.

**Which chats are in RAM, and how many may be** (2026-09-25). A session's
process holds ~450 MB and the box allows four of them, so "is this
conversation resident right now" is a question only this broker can answer
and the app has to be able to ask: the owner wanted the chat list filterable
to the sessions that are actually running. Each record carries ``live`` --
read off the process table (``Session.is_live``), true while a process of
this broker's is behind the session and false for every record off disk --
and the list route carries ``live_count`` and ``cap`` beside the rows, so a
page draws "2 of 4 live" without counting a list that ``?agent=`` may have
narrowed. It is residency and not turn state: a ``done`` session with a warm
idle process is live, a ``waiting`` one is live by definition, and an
``exited`` one is not. ``broker.residency`` on the live list says when the
count moves.

**The agents have names.** ``GET /v1/agents`` answers objects -- ``id``,
``name``, ``face``, read from each persona's ``agent.json`` -- and not the
bare strings it answered until 2026-09-18. A page draws an agent's name and
mark, and both are facts about the agent that only the box can state.

Usage:  session-broker [--addr ADDR] [--port PORT] [--foreground]
"""

